        "history":  "<strong>Historical Context</strong><br><br>In the early 1980s, all home consoles used raster displays — images built from pixels on the television screen. Vector arcade games — Asteroids, Battlezone, Tempest — produced smooth and clear graphics impossible to faithfully reproduce on the raster televisions of the era. Smith Engineering identified the opportunity to bring exactly this vector technology home, with a console that included its own monitor.<br><br><strong>Console Development</strong><br><br>The Vectrex was developed by Smith Engineering, a Santa Monica firm that prioritized creativity and innovation. The Motorola 68A09 processor at 1.5 MHz, 1 KB RAM, and 8 KB ROM drove a 9-inch vector CRT monitor integrated directly into the console — eliminating the dependency on a television. The vector graphics produced extraordinarily clear and pixelation-free images. To compensate for the lack of native color, each game included a colored plastic overlay applied to the screen — an ingenious solution that provided the illusion of color without consuming processor resources. The console was initially marketed by General Consumer Electronics, and later by Milton Bradley.<br><br><strong>Launch</strong><br><br>The Vectrex was launched in October 1982 at a price of 199 USD. The game Mine Storm was included in the console's ROM — an Asteroids clone. The official library reached approximately 28 titles, including faithful ports from Cinematronics and Konami. A notable accessory was the 3-D Imager — the first 3D peripheral for any console, functioning through a rotating disc with filters that synchronized images for each eye. The Light Pen allowed direct drawing on the screen.<br><br><strong>Market Impact</strong><br><br>The Vectrex arrived on the market simultaneously with the 1983 industry crash. The press had harshly criticized the lack of native color, and prices of computers like the Commodore 64 had dropped dramatically, offering much more functionality at similar prices. Mine Storm became famous for a bug at level 13 that froze the system — users who reported the issue received the corrected version, Mine Storm II, for free, today an extremely rare piece. In February 1984, Milton Bradley discontinued the console, generating losses of 31.6 million dollars.<br><strong>Legacy</strong><br><br>The Vectrex remains the only home gaming console to have ever used vector display technology — a unique distinction in the history of the industry. The passionate homebrew community continues to create new games for the platform. The Vectrex Mini project on Kickstarter raised over 1.3 million dollars for a scaled-down version with an AMOLED screen, proving that vector innovation remains relevant even today."
