        "history":  "<strong>Historical Context</strong><br><br>In the early 1980s, Sega was a well-recognized company in the arcade industry, with successful titles in game halls. However, the arcade market was entering a visible decline, and companies were seeking new directions. Under the leadership of Hayao Nakayama, Sega decided to transfer its arcade hardware expertise to a home console, thus entering a new segment — home consoles — against Nintendo, which was already preparing the Famicom.<br><br><strong>Console Development</strong><br><br>The SG-1000 was designed with standard components, a clear choice for cost control and rapid production. The configuration included the Zilog Z80A processor at 3.58 MHz, the Texas Instruments TMS9918A VDP, 1 KB of system RAM, and 16 KB of VRAM. The console delivered a resolution of 256×192, 16 colors, and up to 32 sprites. Sound was handled by the SN76489AN chip. The platform supported two storage formats: ROM cartridges and Sega My Card, the latter requiring the Card Catcher accessory. The library included adapted arcade titles, including the first game by Yuji Naka.<br><br><strong>Launch</strong><br><br>The SG-1000 entered the market on July 15, 1983 — on the same day as Nintendo's Famicom — at a price of 15,000 yen. Sega's internal estimates were cautious, but the initial result exceeded expectations: approximately 160,000 units sold in the first year, compared to a forecast of 50,000. The simultaneous launch with the Famicom turned the SG-1000's debut into a direct confrontation from day one, a battle that Sega had not won.<br><br><strong>Market Impact</strong><br><br>Even though the technical advantage was on Nintendo's side, the SG-1000 demonstrated that Sega could build a relevant product for the home audience. The official presence remained concentrated in Japan, with limited distribution in a few external markets and the appearance of compatible clones. Commercially, the system failed to match the Famicom's strength, and criticisms targeted the more limited graphical capabilities and the less visible library. Nevertheless, the results gave the company the confidence needed to continue investing in consumer hardware.<br><br><strong>Legacy</strong><br><br>The SG-1000 opened Sega's path into the home console market and laid the direct technological foundations for the Master System. The hardware progression — SC-3000, SG-1000 II, Mark III — transformed the SG-1000 into a true starting point for the Sega generations that followed, culminating in the success of the Mega Drive era. For the company's history, the SG-1000 remains the debut moment that made possible the hardware maturity stage in the years that followed."
