        "history":  "<strong>Historical Context</strong><br><br>In the mid-1980s, Nintendo's NES had rapidly gained dominance in the North American and Japanese markets after the 1983 crash. Sega, with the SG-1000 and Mark III launched in Japan, was seeking to conquer Western markets with a technically superior console. Nintendo's exclusive licensing policies limited third-party developers, creating an opportunity for Sega to attract dissatisfied studios and consumers who wanted alternatives.<br><br><strong>Console Development</strong><br><br>The Master System originated from the SG-1000 line, with the real starting point being the Mark III, launched in Japan on October 20, 1985. The internally designed VDP, based on the TMS9918 design and extended in Mode IV, offered a resolution of 256×192 with 32 simultaneous colors from a palette of 64 — richer than the NES. The system handled up to 64 sprites with a limit of 8 per line. Standard sound came from the SN76489, while the Japanese variant could add the YM2413 FM for a distinctly superior audio signature. The console supported ROM cartridges and Sega Cards, a more affordable compact format. Unusual peripherals included the Light Phaser and SegaScope 3-D Glasses.<br><br><strong>Launch</strong><br><br>In 1986, Sega redesigned the platform for the American market under the name Master System, with a visual design oriented toward modern electronics. The white grid packaging was memorable, but the marketing impact fell short of expectations. In 1987, US distribution rights were transferred to Tonka — a company with no experience in electronics — which slowed down the localization of important titles and weakened the platform's commercial positioning. In Europe and Brazil, efficient local distribution produced much better results.<br><br><strong>Market Impact</strong><br><br>Although the Master System's hardware was technically superior to the NES, sales in Japan and North America remained weak due to Nintendo's policies that limited third-party developer support. The console built a strong identity in Europe and especially in Brazil, where the relationship with Tectoy transformed it into a lasting phenomenon with extended long-term production. The Pause button placed on the console — not on the controller — remains a design decision criticized in everyday use.<br><br><strong>Legacy</strong><br><br>The Master System remains an essential platform for understanding Sega's transition from arcade to home consoles. The exceptional longevity in Brazil — where the console was produced and sold for entire decades — demonstrates that commercial success depends as much on distribution and local adaptation as on hardware quality. The console prepared the technical and commercial ground for the success of the Mega Drive era."
